license: MIT
abstract: >-
  HelioFleet is a web tool that shows where heliophysics spacecraft (SDO,
  SOHO, Aditya-L1, ACE, Wind, DSCOVR, IMAP, SWFO-L1, Carruthers, Solar
  Orbiter, Parker Solar Probe,
  BepiColombo, STEREO-A/B and MAVEN) are relative to the Sun and Earth on
  any date since 2010. It reports Stonyhurst and Carrington coordinates and
  Parker-spiral footpoints, searches for radial alignments, quadratures and
  magnetic connections, checks which observers see a given point on the Sun,
  and exports coordinates and time series. It draws the fields of view of
  coronagraphs and heliospheric imagers, summarises which solar longitudes
  are imaged, and shows flares, CMEs and geomagnetic storms from NASA DONKI.
  A sky view projects every instrument's field of view onto the plane of the
  sky seen from Earth or from a chosen spacecraft, with MLSO K-Cor and UCoMP
  fields and DKIST Cryo-NIRSP observation footprints. Positions come from
  JPL Horizons ephemerides, refreshed monthly. The interface is available in
  English, Hindi and Spanish.
keywords:
  - heliophysics
  - spacecraft ephemerides
  - solar physics
  - multi-spacecraft observations
  - solar eclipse
  - space weather
  - JPL Horizons
  - coronagraph
  - DKIST
